<p>Violent protests broke out in Karachi and elsewhere in Pakistan after reports said Iran’s supreme leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, was killed in a U.S.-Israeli strike on Tehran. In Karachi, crowds tried to storm the U.S. consulate compound as demonstrators clashed with police near the diplomatic site. Security forces used crowd-control measures as the confrontation escalated. The unrest turned deadly nationwide. Multiple outlets said at least 22 people were killed and many more were wounded. Officials said security forces eventually brought the Karachi situation under control, as governments across the region braced for wider instability and possible fallout from any broader conflict.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/al8zUx0GRJwEse7zdI2z5YMFiq8Fc7T5Hv6YFTy9AL79sw7-QiRq1ivncnSyP38_sjpavx1oxCddG-qpAa3FrMaOlfNwHOykBkfCuhAZYNvFfysqMEn7fCf4p7XV_FwUaKsJmqLhDj_75avxbtYw2Y6PlOs_7GWwb6bgXKSO3wpL261pBO8C-fqOXR8rvqYI-0o4nDuJax1Wh8E' alt='Protesters gather amid unrest linked to regional tensions, according to reporting.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>How large the Karachi clash got: AP said hundreds of protesters tried to storm the U.S. consulate in Karachi.</li><li>Karachi casualty toll (Sindh officials): A Sindh government spokesperson said 10 protesters were killed and 34 were injured outside the U.S. consulate.</li><li>Other Karachi estimates: One report quoted officials saying at least nine people were killed and about 25 were injured, several critically.</li><li>Authorities’ account: A Sindh government spokesperson said police reached the site and brought the situation under control.</li><li>Rescue response: Faisal Edhi of the Edhi Foundation rescue service was among those responding in the aftermath in Karachi.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sindh provincial government: A Sindh government spokesperson said security forces opened fire as protesters tried to storm the consulate gates, and later said police brought the situation under control. (<a href